### 4\. Machine Learning strategy

I recommend building three models and comparing them rather than selecting XGBoost immediately.

### Prediction problem

Our initial ML experiment will be:

> Given historical seismic activity in a geographic grid cell, estimate the probability of at least one earthquake of magnitude 4.5 or greater occurring in that cell during the following 30 days.

This is a probabilistic forecasting experiment, not a claim of reliable earthquake prediction.

Feature engineering

For each geographic cell and monthly observation date:

| Feature               | Meaning                    |
| --------------------- | -------------------------- |
| events_last_30d       | Events in previous 30 days |
| events_last_90d       | Events in previous 90 days |
| events_last_365d      | Events in previous year    |
| mean_magnitude_90d    | Average magnitude          |
| max_magnitude_90d     | Maximum magnitude          |
| mean_depth_90d        | Average depth              |
| days_since_last_event | Time since last event      |
| latitude_center       | Grid center latitude       |
| longitude_center      | Grid center longitude      |

We will calculate the target using future observations only:

`target = 1` if at least one qualifying event occurs in the following 30 days; otherwise `0`.

### Models to compare

| Model               | Purpose             |
| ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Logistic Regression | Baseline classifier |
| Random Forest       | Nonlinear patterns  |
| XGBoost             | Advanced tabular ML |

We should also include a simple historical-rate baseline, such as a Poisson model.

Important: Earthquakes are rare and spatially clustered. We must account for class imbalance, use chronological train/validation/test splits, and avoid random row splitting that leaks future information.

### Model evaluation

* Precision and Recall.

* F1-score.

* PR-AUC.

* ROC-AUC.

* Brier Score.

* Calibration curve.

* False-positive and false-negative analysis.

For a probabilistic model, calibration and Brier Score are particularly important. A model that says 20% should produce approximately 20% events across comparable predictions.

We should also compare performance across different geographic regions and time periods.

